# vmsync benchmark harness configuration.
#
# Copy this file to bench.conf (untracked, see .gitignore in this
# directory -- it will contain SSH connection details) and fill in your own
# environment. bench.sh refuses to run for real until
# I_UNDERSTAND_THIS_IS_DESTRUCTIVE is set to "yes" below.
#
# This is a plain shell file, sourced directly by bench.sh -- normal shell
# quoting rules apply.

# --- What to test ------------------------------------------------------

# Path to the vmsync binary under test.
VMSYNC_BIN=/usr/local/bin/vmsync

# Set to "yes" if bench.sh itself runs directly on the source hypervisor
# (e.g. SOURCE_URI is a local "qemu:///system" URI, not qemu+ssh://).
# When "yes", the harness's own direct shell-outs against SOURCE_HOST
# (currently just: removing a leftover external-snapshot overlay file in
# Stage 4) run locally instead of over ssh_host_cmd -- no SSH access to
# the source host is needed in that case. Has no effect on vmsync's own
# -ssh-* flags, which are always for TARGET_URI's required qemu+ssh://
# transport (and -use-ssh bridging), independent of this setting. There
# is no equivalent TARGET_LOCAL: the target must stay reachable over SSH,
# since vmsync itself requires -target-uri to be qemu+ssh://.
SOURCE_LOCAL=yes

# libvirt URIs, exactly as you'd pass to vmsync's own -source-uri/-target-uri.
# -target-uri MUST be qemu+ssh:// (vmsync itself requires this).
#SOURCE_URI="qemu+ssh://root@source-host.example.org/system"
SOURCE_URI="qemu:///system"
TARGET_URI="qemu+ssh://root@target-host.example.org/system"

# Bare hostnames (no scheme/user), used by the harness's OWN direct ssh
# calls (qemu-io tampering, removing a leftover snapshot overlay file) --
# normally the same hosts as the URIs above.
SOURCE_HOST=source-host.example.org
TARGET_HOST=target-host.example.org

# The VM to test. This harness syncs SOURCE_DOMAIN to TARGET_DOMAIN
# repeatedly, deletes and recreates the target replica many times (-reinit),
# and deliberately corrupts the target replica's disk during verify-mode
# testing (always healed with a full resync immediately after). Use a
# disposable/dedicated test VM -- never a real, in-use replication target.
SOURCE_DOMAIN=vmsync-bench-vm
TARGET_DOMAIN=vmsync-bench-vm

# Recommended: always set this. Without it, target disk path resolution
# depends on the source having no active external snapshot at sync time
# (see pkg/util.SetTargetPath's own doc comment) -- setting this removes
# that ambiguity entirely, and is required for this harness's own
# virsh-dumpxml-based disk path lookup (used for tampering) to be reliable.
TARGET_DISK_PATH=/data/vmsync-bench

# --- SSH ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared between vmsync's own -ssh-* flags and the harness's own direct ssh
# calls to SOURCE_HOST/TARGET_HOST.

SSH_USER=root
SSH_KEY=/root/.ssh/id_vmsync_bench
SSH_PORT=22
SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS=""

# --- vmsync flags that apply to every invocation ------------------------

# Stage 1 (the transport matrix) always overrides -io-depth per
# combination from scenarios.conf's own [iodepth] section -- this value is
# only actually used as the fixed -io-depth for Stage 2 (verify+tamper)
# and Stage 3 (reinit-after-failures), which don't vary it.
IO_DEPTH=8
BRIDGE_HELPER_PATH=""   # empty = vmsync's own default (/usr/local/bin/vmsync-bridge-helper)

# What -reinit does with the target disk it is about to replace: "delete" or
# "rename". This harness defaults to delete; vmsync itself defaults to rename.
#
# vmsync is right to rename by default -- the target of a -reinit may be a
# former primary whose disks still hold everything written after the last
# successful sync, and that is unrecoverable once deleted. None of that applies
# to the disposable test VM this harness requires, which it deletes and
# recreates dozens of times per run regardless.
#
# What does apply is the cost: every -reinit leaves a full-size aside copy that
# is never reaped automatically, and this harness reinits constantly -- once per
# verify sub-test, once per heal, four times in Stage 8 alone. On a multi-GB
# disk that fills TARGET_DISK_PATH partway through a long run, and it surfaces
# as a confusing mid-stage sync failure rather than as "out of disk".
#
# Set it to "rename" if you would rather keep every replaced copy and have the
# space for them.
REPLACED_DISK_ACTION=delete

# Transport flags added to every sync in every stage EXCEPT Stage 1.
# Default: -compress.
#
# Stage 1 is the one stage measuring transport, so it chooses its own from
# scenarios.conf and is never given these. Every other stage copies a disk
# only so there is something real to tamper with, reinit, snapshot, promote
# or fence -- the transport is incidental there, and the fastest way across
# the wire is simply the least waiting. On a link that is the bottleneck (a
# saturated 1GbE reads as roughly 110 MB/s in results.csv) that is most of
# their runtime, and Stage 6 alone does three full copies.
#
# This does not change what those stages test: vmsync's port allocator
# handles "whichever combination of -compress/-netbuffer/-verify is active"
# by design, reserving 4N target ports when both are on, so the verify modes
# compose with the bridge rather than working around it.
#
# -compress requires vmsync-bridge-helper on the TARGET host. Without it
# every affected stage fails at its baseline -- and says so, naming this
# setting. Set it to "" to turn it off, or to any vmsync transport flags,
# e.g. "-compress=zstd -compress-level 3" or "-compress -netbuffer 128k,1G".
#BENCH_SYNC_ARGS="-compress"

# --- Verify + tamper test ------------------------------------------------

# Which disk (libvirt <target dev='...'>) to tamper for the -verify
# correctness tests.
TAMPER_DISK_DEV=vda

# EVERY byte count in this section is a plain decimal number. qemu-io accepts
# k/M/G suffixes and an older version of this file advertised them, but the
# harness now does arithmetic on these values to place a random corruption,
# and $(( 100M )) is a bash syntax error that ends the run.

# random: each tamper picks its own offset and length (default).
# fixed:  every tamper uses TAMPER_OFFSET/TAMPER_LENGTH below, as before.
#
# Random placement finds what a single hand-picked offset cannot: a corruption
# the compare happens to skip, a boundary case in the mismatch scanner, an
# offset whose detection depends on where it falls. It is only useful because
# it is reproducible -- see TAMPER_SEED.
TAMPER_MODE=random

# The band a random offset is drawn from. Keep it inside the disk's allocated,
# real data region: well past the partition table and boot sectors, and not out
# in never-written space, so the write is a genuine content change rather than
# a hole the compare has no reason to look at. TAMPER_BAND_END=0 means "up to
# the disk's virtual size", read off the image at run time.
TAMPER_BAND_START=67108864   # 64MiB in
TAMPER_BAND_END=0

# The size range a random tamper is drawn from.
#
# The 64KiB floor is not caution, it is arithmetic. vmsync reports mismatches
# at a 4096-byte granularity, so anything smaller than that is indistinguishable
# from a 4KiB tamper. And -verify=online discards a whole reported range if ANY
# byte of it overlaps a region the guest wrote during the compare window, where
# those regions come from a dirty bitmap at qemu's default 64KiB granularity --
# so a tamper smaller than one granule can be swallowed whole by an unrelated
# guest write and read as "verify missed it".
TAMPER_LENGTH_MIN=65536
TAMPER_LENGTH_MAX=262144
TAMPER_ALIGN=4096

# The fill pattern. Must be non-zero: a zero fill is a content no-op wherever
# the source already reads as zero, and undetectable by every verify mode by
# design.
TAMPER_PATTERN=0xAA

# Seed for the random placement. Empty means "derive it from the run id", which
# is logged at startup and printed with every failure. Re-run with
# TAMPER_SEED=<that value> to replay a run's corruptions byte for byte.
#
# This is what makes random placement worth having. A failure at an offset you
# cannot reproduce is a failure you cannot investigate.
#TAMPER_SEED=

# Used only when TAMPER_MODE=fixed. Same guidance as TAMPER_BAND_START above:
# somewhere with real data in it. Note the default length is below the 64KiB
# floor explained above, and is kept only for continuity with older configs.
TAMPER_OFFSET=104857600   # 100MiB in
TAMPER_LENGTH=4096

# --- verify after a long incremental chain (Stage 8, --stages ...,verify-long) --
#
# Every other stage verifies a replica built moments ago by a single -reinit.
# This one builds it the way a real deployment does -- dozens of incremental
# syncs, each carrying real guest writes -- and only then asks whether
# corruption is still detectable. It is the longest stage by a wide margin and
# is opt-in for that reason.
#
# It needs the guest to actually write between copies, or the chain is just
# VERIFY_LONG_COPIES no-ops against an empty dirty bitmap and the stage proves
# nothing. That is what GUEST_DIRTY below is for; without it the stage SKIPS
# rather than reporting a green result that verified nothing.

# Incremental syncs per MODE, not per stage. Each mode gets its own fresh
# chain, because healing a tamper needs a full -reinit and that destroys the
# chain -- so one chain followed by several tamper/verify attempts would test a
# deep chain exactly once and a one-deep chain every time after, while
# reporting all of them as if they had tested the same thing.
#
# The corruption is injected after a RANDOM copy within the chain rather than
# after the last one, so the copies that follow run over the damage the way
# they would in production. That asks a question the end-of-chain version
# cannot: does the corruption survive the incremental syncs that follow it, and
# is it still detectable afterwards?
#
# Budget roughly VERIFY_LONG_MODES x (VERIFY_LONG_COPIES + 1) syncs, plus one
# final heal -- 64 with these defaults, three of them full resyncs. Trim
# VERIFY_LONG_MODES to a single mode if that is too much for one sitting.
VERIFY_LONG_COPIES=20
# fast/full/qemu-img -- the names -verify actually takes. This line used to say
# "compare fast online", two of which were renamed out of vmsync some time ago,
# and bench.sh validates the list at startup: copying this file as the missing-
# config error tells you to therefore died on its own example.
VERIFY_LONG_MODES="fast full qemu-img"

# --- dirtying the guest's own disk ------------------------------------------
#
# There is no virsh verb for running a command inside a guest; this goes through
# the QEMU guest agent's guest-exec RPC.
#
# TWO separate things must be true, and the first does not imply the second: the
# agent has to be running (vmsync's own FSFreeze already needs that), AND
# guest-exec must not be blocked. RHEL-family packages ship
# /etc/sysconfig/qemu-ga with guest-exec and guest-file-* listed in BLOCK_RPCS
# by default, so an agent that happily freezes filesystems will still refuse to
# run dd. Remove it from that list and restart qemu-guest-agent. The harness
# probes for this and says so rather than failing obscurely mid-stage.
GUEST_DIRTY=yes

# Always the same path, rewritten in place. That keeps the guest's block
# allocation stable, so every round dirties a comparable number of blocks and
# the per-round transferred_bytes in results.csv is a flat line any deviation
# stands out against. A fresh file per round would grow the image monotonically
# across twenty rounds instead.
GUEST_DIRTY_PATH=/var/tmp/vmsync-bench-dirty
GUEST_DIRTY_MIB=64

# How long to wait for each in-guest write to finish. guest-exec is
# asynchronous -- it returns a pid immediately -- so the harness polls
# guest-exec-status rather than letting the next checkpoint be taken while dd
# is still running.
#GUEST_DIRTY_TIMEOUT=120

# --- reinit-after-failures test ------------------------------------------

# How many induced failures to simulate before expecting a forced reinit.
REINIT_AFTER_FAILURES_N=3

# --- DefineDomain rollback test (Stage 5b, opt-in via --stages ...,define) --

# How long (seconds) Stage 5b will wait for a full sync to reach the point
# where DefineDomain starts undefining the target before giving up on
# timing its disruption for that run (scored as a SKIP, not a FAIL, if this
# elapses first). Raise this if your test VM's disk is large enough that a
# full -reinit sync legitimately takes longer than the default.
DEFINE_ROLLBACK_WAIT_SECONDS=300

# --- Failover test (Stage 6, opt-in via --stages ...,failover) --------------

# Path to the vmsync binary ON THE TARGET HOST.
#
# Needed because -promote and -update-role refuse a remote libvirt URI by
# design: a failover has to work when the other site is unreachable, so it
# runs where the domain lives and needs no credentials to reach anywhere
# else. This harness therefore has to invoke vmsync over SSH on the target
# rather than driving it from here as every other stage does.
#
# Leave it unset to skip Stage 6 entirely (it skips with a message rather
# than failing).
#TARGET_VMSYNC_BIN=/usr/local/bin/vmsync


# --- Fencing end to end with real agents (Stage 7, --stages ...,fence-agent) --
#
# WARNING: this stage STOPS THE SOURCE VM. It is the only stage that touches
# the source's power state, and it has to -- a fence only ever acts on a
# running domain, so a fence that never fires would prove nothing. It starts
# the source again afterwards and clears both roles, but read Stage 7's own
# comment in bench.sh before enabling it.

# vmsync-agent on the SOURCE host. This is the agent that gets fenced.
# Required for Stage 7; unset skips it.
#SOURCE_AGENT_BIN=/usr/local/bin/vmsync-agent

# vmsync-agent on the TARGET host. Optional: when set, Stage 7 also starts an
# agent there and asserts it does NOT fence the promoted copy (a fence sweep
# must skip anything whose role is not source).
#TARGET_AGENT_BIN=/usr/local/bin/vmsync-agent

# Path to vmsync on the SOURCE host, for the agent to shell out to. Defaults
# to VMSYNC_BIN, which is right whenever SOURCE_LOCAL=yes.
#SOURCE_VMSYNC_BIN=/usr/local/bin/vmsync

# Scratch directory on each host for the throwaway standalone agent's config,
# state dir and log. Removed by nothing -- inspect agent.log there when a
# fence does not fire.
#AGENT_WORK_DIR=/var/tmp/vmsync-bench-agent

# How long to wait for the fence to shut the source down. The agent sweeps
# once immediately at startup, so this mostly covers the guest's own
# shutdown, not the sweep interval.
#FENCE_WAIT_SECONDS=180

# How long to then wait for the agent to finish RECORDING that fence.
#
# A separate wait, because the domain going shut off does not mean the agent
# is done: it writes its ledger record as state=running before shelling out to
# vmsync, and rewrites it to done only once that process exits -- and vmsync
# goes on to pause replication after the shutdown. So the shutdown is observed
# while the record still says running, and reading it at that instant reports
# a perfectly good fence as a failure. Seconds, normally.
#FENCE_LEDGER_WAIT_SECONDS=60

# --- Output --------------------------------------------------------------

RESULT_DIR=./results

# --- Safety gate -----------------------------------------------------------
# Must be "yes" to run anything for real. Use bench.sh --dry-run to see
# what would run (every vmsync command line, no ssh/qemu-io/vmsync calls
# actually made) without touching this.
# While not modifying source VM, it will totally tamper with target VM,
# perhaps keep open processes / port / file descriptors on source and target,
# perhaps even nuke your whole source / target machines,
# replicate itself into skynet and take over the world.
# Only run this if you fully understand it's implications.

I_UNDERSTAND_THIS_IS_DESTRUCTIVE=no
